WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.358
Here is a question people are often too polite to ask.

2
00:00:06.358 --> 00:00:09.610
Surely the job is done when the code is written.

3
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:10.016
Right?

4
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:12.331
Let's answer that one in a kitchen.

5
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:15.468
Picture yourself baking bread in your home kitchen.

6
00:00:15.468 --> 00:00:16.792
The neighbours love it.

7
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.169
Then the café down the street asks for forty loaves, every morning, starting Monday.

8
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.255
Baking one good loaf was never the hard part.

9
00:00:25.255 --> 00:00:30.776
The hard part is forty good loaves, every morning, while you keep improving the recipe.

10
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.969
And nobody gets a burnt one.

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.798
Software has the same problem.

12
00:00:35.798 --> 00:00:41.671
A program helps nobody until it runs for real people, every day, after every change.

13
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.755
Now you bake, and your cousin drives the loaves to the café.

14
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:49.664
One Friday, the café calls at seven.

15
00:00:49.664 --> 00:00:51.012
The bread is flat.

16
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.799
Only you know the recipe.

17
00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:56.495
So you blame the hot van, and your cousin blames the dough.

18
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.617
And the café?

19
00:00:57.617 --> 00:00:58.675
Still no bread.

20
00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:02.203
The only thing that arrived on time was the blame.

21
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:06.771
So the point is: with a wall in the middle, nobody owns the whole road.

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.055
Software teams were split the same way for years.

23
00:01:10.055 --> 00:01:13.733
Developers wrote the code, the instructions for a computer.

24
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:20.437
Operations ran it on servers, computers that stay on day and night and answer other computers.

25
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.478
At two in the morning the app broke, and operations got the call.

26
00:01:24.478 --> 00:01:26.824
But only developers understood the code.

27
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:32.117
So releases, batches of changes for users, became rare, huge and dreaded.

28
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:37.289
Wouldn't it be nice if the person who knows the recipe also heard about the flat bread?

29
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.816
That wish has a name.

30
00:01:38.816 --> 00:01:44.767
DevOps: a way of working where the people who build software also share the work of running it.

31
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.780
You share one goal, good bread at seven, and take turns on the early call.

32
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.072
That's on-call duty.

33
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.743
You make small changes, taste every batch, and buy the timer and mixer that make those habits cheap.

34
00:01:57.743 --> 00:01:59.612
Culture, practices and tools.

35
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:01.527
Notice the order, though.

36
00:02:01.527 --> 00:02:04.711
A shiny new mixer never got two people talking.

37
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:06.868
Tools make good habits cheap.

38
00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.403
They don't create them.

39
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:11.578
Now follow one change down the road.

40
00:02:11.578 --> 00:02:13.635
Say you decide to use less sugar.

41
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:17.726
You write it on the recipe card, bake a test batch and taste it.

42
00:02:17.726 --> 00:02:20.259
Then the bag, the van, and the café shelf.

43
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.292
Now picture a busy Tuesday.

44
00:02:22.292 --> 00:02:25.131
You skip the tasting, to save ten minutes.

45
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.884
On Friday, an unhappy café owner tells you the bread tastes of nothing.

46
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.216
The mistake was made on Tuesday.

47
00:02:32.216 --> 00:02:35.191
You paid for it on Friday, far down the road.

48
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.111
Hold on to that distance.

49
00:02:37.111 --> 00:02:39.232
It is the whole story of today.

50
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:47.521
Software takes the same road, called the pipeline: fixed steps, run by machines, from your laptop to real users.

51
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:48.095
Code.

52
00:02:48.095 --> 00:02:53.955
You change code, and commit it to git, a tool that saves snapshots of your files.

53
00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:54.585
Build.

54
00:02:54.585 --> 00:02:58.276
A machine turns the code into something that can run.

55
00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:58.840
Test.

56
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.399
Small programs check that the change works, and broke nothing else.

57
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.166
Package.

58
00:03:04.166 --> 00:03:09.117
The app and everything it needs go into one bundle, a container image.

59
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:09.898
Release.

60
00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:14.488
It gets a version number, and goes to a shared store, a registry.

61
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.226
Deploy.

62
00:03:15.226 --> 00:03:19.129
The new version replaces the old one on the servers.

63
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:19.627
Run.

64
00:03:19.627 --> 00:03:24.040
Software keeps the app alive, and restarts it if it crashes.

65
00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:24.858
Observe.

66
00:03:24.858 --> 00:03:28.483
Graphs, logs and alerts show how the app behaves.

67
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.319
Feedback.

68
00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.935
What you learn shapes the next change.

69
00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:33.773
And the loop starts again.

70
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.113
The bakery, mapped: the recipe card is the code, the tasting is the test, the bag is the package, and the van is the deploy.

71
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:45.128
Let's send one tiny change down this road.

72
00:03:45.128 --> 00:03:51.584
Tomorrow you create hello-devops, a tiny web app that greets every visitor and counts the visits.

73
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.652
You change its greeting from Hello, DevOps!

74
00:03:54.652 --> 00:03:56.112
to Hi there, and push.

75
00:03:56.112 --> 00:03:59.031
But one test still expects the old greeting.

76
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:01.757
Where on the road does this mistake get caught?

77
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:07.087
Right here, at the test step.

78
00:04:07.087 --> 00:04:08.329
It fails at once.

79
00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:15.196
You find out two minutes later, on your own branch, your own line of work, instead of from a user on Friday night.

80
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.468
Remember the tasting we skipped?

81
00:04:17.468 --> 00:04:21.280
This is that tasting, done by a machine, every single time.

82
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.819
Every step catches its own kind of mistake, as close as possible to where it was made.

83
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:30.240
Fix the test, push again, and the change moves on.

84
00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:34.948
Steps two to six have a name you will see everywhere: CI/CD.

85
00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:41.403
Continuous integration: small changes, added often, each built and tested by a machine.

86
00:04:41.403 --> 00:04:46.134
Continuous delivery: every change that passes is ready to release.

87
00:04:46.134 --> 00:04:47.496
Part 6 builds both.

88
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.710
So, should a machine do everything?

89
00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:50.917
No.

90
00:04:50.917 --> 00:04:53.603
Deciding what to change takes a person.

91
00:04:53.603 --> 00:04:56.220
Building, testing and shipping do not.

92
00:04:56.433 --> 00:05:00.131
When a step goes the same way every time, give it to a machine.

93
00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.974
So, is the job done when the code is written?

94
00:05:03.974 --> 00:05:04.184
No.

95
00:05:04.184 --> 00:05:06.976
Finished code is only the halfway point.

96
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.372
You are done when it runs well in production, the live system your users use, and you can see that it does.

97
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.389
One team owns the whole road.

98
00:05:15.389 --> 00:05:17.486
Each step catches its own mistake.

99
00:05:17.486 --> 00:05:19.892
And whatever repeats goes to a machine.

100
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:21.815
A quick check.

101
00:05:21.815 --> 00:05:27.961
Your team has a script that builds and tests the app, but someone runs it by hand once a week.

102
00:05:27.961 --> 00:05:28.941
Best next step?

103
00:05:32.167 --> 00:05:33.567
Run it on every push.

104
00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:36.767
Feedback in minutes beats a tasting once a week.
